Stop waiting for a frame callback once the Wayland connection has failed.

Wayland_GLES_SwapWindow waits for the previous commit's frame callback on its
own event queue, with a timeout so a compositor that stops sending them cannot
stall the caller. The timeout is only checked on the path that reads the
socket. When wl_display_prepare_read_queue() fails because events are already
queued, the loop dispatches them and starts over. After the connection fails,
wl_display_dispatch_queue_pending() returns -1 without dispatching anything,
the queue never empties, and the loop spins forever at full CPU.

That is what happens on the MLP1 when HDMI is unplugged during a RetroArch
game. Weston loses its only output and jawakad restarts it, and if a frame
callback was read into the queue just before the socket closed, RetroArch hangs
in this loop. It ignores SIGTERM because its main loop never runs again, so it
can only be killed, which loses SRAM just as the crash 0001 fixes does. Sampled
on the device: the main thread cycles through wl_display_flush,
wl_display_prepare_read_queue and wl_display_dispatch_queue_pending inside
Wayland_GLES_SwapWindow. The same loop is unchanged in SDL2 and SDL3 upstream.

Nothing in the loop can make progress once the display has an error, so leave
it then. The next Wayland_PumpEvents() sees the same error and sends SDL_QUIT,
and RetroArch exits normally and writes SRAM.

diff --git a/src/video/wayland/SDL_waylandopengles.c b/src/video/wayland/SDL_waylandopengles.c
index b18e024..7e4b980 100644
--- a/src/video/wayland/SDL_waylandopengles.c
+++ b/src/video/wayland/SDL_waylandopengles.c
@@ -130,6 +130,14 @@ int Wayland_GLES_SwapWindow(_THIS, SDL_Window *window)
         while (SDL_AtomicGet(&data->swap_interval_ready) == 0) {
             Uint32 now;
 
+            /* Once the connection has failed, queued events are never dispatched,
+             * so the pending-events path below would spin forever without
+             * reaching the timeout. Wayland_PumpEvents() turns the failure into
+             * SDL_QUIT. */
+            if (WAYLAND_wl_display_get_error(display) != 0) {
+                break;
+            }
+
             WAYLAND_wl_display_flush(display);
 
             /* wl_display_prepare_read_queue() will return -1 if the event queue is not empty.
